<html>
<head>
<title>Consultation Letter Template</title>
<!--
  Only placeholders that ALWAYS resolve to a non-empty value belong in a template.
  populateTemplate() in editControl2.js falls back to prompt() for any placeholder whose
  cached value is empty, so one that is merely often-populated interrupts the clinician
  with a dialog on every new letter. The referring block is the trap: it is empty for any
  patient with no referring doctor on file, which is most of them, so it is deliberately
  NOT in this template. The "Referring Block" sidebar button inserts it on demand.
  (Placeholder syntax is avoided in this comment on purpose - the editor only reads the
  body, but a stray marker here would still mislead the next author.)
-->
<meta http-equiv="Content-Type" content="text/html; charset=UTF-8">
<style type="text/css">
body {
    font-size: 12pt;
    font-family: "Times New Roman", Times, serif;
    background-color: #FFFFFF;
    margin: 1em;
}
</style>
<style type="text/css" media="print">
* { color: #000000; }
.DoNotPrint { display: none; }
@page { margin: 2cm; }
</style>
</head>
<body contenteditable onLoad="document.designMode = 'on';">
##letterhead##
<p>##today##</p>
<p><b>Re:</b> ##label##</p>
<p>Dear Doctor,</p>
<p>Thank you for asking me to see ##first_last_name##, a ##ageGender##.</p>
<p><br></p>
<p><b>History</b><br></p>
<p><b>Examination</b><br></p>
<p><b>Impression</b><br></p>
<p><b>Plan</b><br></p>
##_ClosingSalutation##
</body>
</html>
